  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three platform families for creating artificial gauge fields for photons. At the center is the idea of “AGF for photons,” with three branches showing how photonic motion and interactions can be engineered in different quantum electrodynamics settings.\n\nOne branch illustrates cavity QED: light bounces between mirrors and photons interact through atoms placed in the cavity. The sketch suggests that atom-mediated photon-photon interactions can produce strongly correlated optical states, including a photonic Laughlin state.\n\nA second branch shows waveguide QED, where quantum emitters are coupled to a long waveguide. An incoming optical signal interacts with the emitters, leading to reflected and transmitted light. This conveys how guided photons can be controlled through emitter-waveguide coupling and scattering.\n\nThe third branch depicts circuit QED using superconducting circuits. A chip-like lattice and a smaller loop threaded by a magnetic-field symbol suggest engineered couplings and synthetic magnetic flux for microwave photons in circuit architectures.\n\nTaken together, the figure compares cavity, waveguide, and circuit implementations as complementary routes to manipulate photons as interacting quantum particles and to realize gauge-field-like behavior in optical or microwave systems.",
